NVIDIA demonstrates Microduck robot training with Isaac Lab 3.0, presenting a workflow for teaching a small, open-source bipedal robot to walk in simulation before deploying the learned movements to physical hardware. Developed by Pollen Robotics and Hugging Face, Microduck can be trained using NVIDIA Isaac Lab and Newton. The workflow simulates ground friction, motor delays, battery voltage variations and mechanical play in robot joints. Developers can train walking policies across thousands of parallel GPU simulations, evaluate performance on different terrains and export trained policies in ONNX format. These policies can then run on a physical Microduck robot. NVIDIA also describes how developers can share their models through Hugging Face Hub and evaluate them using Microduck Arena.

